From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jayden Milne <gnss-share@jayd.ml>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 16:11:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] qrtr: add an AF_QIPCRTR socket and a name service client

QRTR is the IPC router that Qualcomm SoCs use to reach services on their
remote processors (modem, audio and sensor DSPs). Go's syscall package
has no sockaddr for AF_QIPCRTR, so the socket uses the raw socket system
calls (numbers from golang.org/x/sys/unix, which every Linux architecture
has) on a non-blocking descriptor registered with Go's poller: Close
interrupts a blocked read, and a send that QRTR flow control holds back
times out.

The control packet layout and constants are those of Linux's
include/uapi/linux/qrtr.h. A lookup goes to the kernel's name service
(net/qrtr/ns.c), which lists the matching servers, ends the listing with
an empty NEW_SERVER, and then reports servers that appear and disappear
for as long as the socket is open.

The Linux tests publish and look up a service number that no remote
processor offers, and skip where AF_QIPCRTR is not available.
